# Discriminated Unions (The Ultimate Pattern) 🏆

A **Discriminated Union** is a union of object types where each type shares a common literal property (the **discriminant**):

```typescript
interface LoadingState {
  status: "loading"; // Discriminant literal!
}

interface SuccessState {
  status: "success"; // Discriminant literal!
  data: string[];
}

interface ErrorState {
  status: "error";   // Discriminant literal!
  error: string;
}

type NetworkState = LoadingState | SuccessState | ErrorState;
```

# Handling Discriminated Unions with Switch 🎯

When switching on the discriminant property, TypeScript automatically narrows the object inside each case block:

```typescript
function renderState(state: NetworkState) {
  switch (state.status) {
    case "loading":
      console.log("⏳ Loading data...");
      break;
    case "success":
      console.log(`✅ Loaded ${state.data.length} items`);
      break;
    case "error":
      console.log(`❌ Failed: ${state.error}`);
      break;
  }
}

renderState({ status: "loading" });
renderState({ status: "success", data: ["Article 1", "Article 2"] });
```

